Long ago, far away in a remote region of Siberia, a small community lived in their own village. They lived in peace, cultivated the land, and hunted, just like the inhabitants of the nearby villages.

The village community was led by a chief named Samil. He was known as a fair and good man. No one had anything bad to say about him. He respected other people, whether they were young or old, men or women, rich or poor. Everyone wanted to be in Samil's company and to be like him.

Then, unfortunately, harder times came and food began to run out for all the inhabitants of the region. In Samil's village, however, everyone was taken care of, and no one had to suffer from hunger. Meanwhile, tensions began to arise between the other villages, and eventually Samil's village also came under threat. The men of a village farther away had noticed that there was still enough food in Samil's village, and they became jealous. The chief of their village was not a good man. He was frightening and infamous, and he caused others to behave unpredictably as well. The warriors of this village began approaching Samil's once peaceful community. Their intention was to come and plunder the village's food and other necessary goods.

Chief Samil's village had to quickly pack their belongings and flee. They took only the necessary items and enough food to last for the duration of their escape. During their flight they camped in a place that was safe and far enough from the marauding band of robbers.

However, while in the new place, their provisions began to decrease, for they had also helped other less fortunate people they encountered along the way. Because of this, Samil gave the order that food must be rationed, and everyone was given their own share equally.

For some time, everything went well. However, one day the chief was informed that food had been stolen from their common storage. The culprit was not found, and so the chief decided to announce to everyone in the village: "Anyone who steals from our common food supply will be punished severely. The thief will be flogged in the middle of the village square before everyone's eyes. There will be no exception."

A few days passed peacefully, and the people were content with their lives and were already thinking about returning to their home village. After a couple of weeks, however, news was again brought to the chief. One of the men responsible for the village's common food came to him. He was uneasy and shifted nervously in fear. He said to the chief, "I now have both good and bad news for you. The good news is that we have finally caught the thief, for he stole again from our common food. Unfortunately, I also have the bad news." Chief Samil wondered at the man's reaction and asked him to tell what the bad news was and who had stolen from them. The bearer of the news looked down at the ground and said quietly, "The thief is your own old mother." The chief was shocked by the news. He had not expected such a report and did not know how he could now act. He had strictly ordered that the thief be flogged. However, he had not taken into account that flogging would likely lead to the death of an elderly person. And now that old person, the thief, was his own beloved mother. The chief thought intensely about what he would now do regarding the punishment. If he were to pardon his own mother, all his authority and credibility would be gone, and no one would respect or obey him anymore. What on earth could he do? How could he possibly get out of this miserable situation and likely save his mother's life?

Think briefly about what you would do if you were that village chief. Then continue reading to hear the astonishing conclusion.

Chief Samil thought deeply about the different options. He could not lie, deceive, or leave the punishment unfulfilled. Samil was a loving leader to all, but at the same time completely just and impartial. Therefore, he came to the conclusion that there was no other option but to punish the guilty one. So he set a day when the punishment would be carried out.

At last the day appointed by Samil arrived, when the punishment would be executed. The chief sat in his chair in the village square, for he did not have the strength to stand, being broken by grief. His face was pale as he watched his own mother being dragged to the square to receive the flogging. The old mother was bound by her hands to the punishment post, and the guard soldier who would carry out the flogging stood ready. He raised the whip and was prepared for the first stroke. Nine more would follow after that.

Just before the first lash, Samil rose from his chair. He could no longer endure the situation and shouted to the soldier, "Stop!" Everyone looked in astonishment at the chief, who stepped forward toward his mother bound in the middle of the square. Samil ordered that his mother's hands be untied and that she be set free. After that, he removed his own chief's battle garment and shirt. He knelt down on the ground in his mother's place and ordered the guard to flog him instead of her. Samil took upon himself the punishment that belonged to the guilty and suffered the penalty meant for another. He nearly died after enduring the punishment on behalf of his mother.

The whole village now understood even more clearly Samil's love toward everyone, but even more clearly they understood what justice means. After this, Samil was respected even more. At last the entire village was able to return to their own home, and the harvest of the following summer once again brought food for all.

This is how true love acts selflessly. God showed us humans such great love, because He is love. He proved it when He suffered the punishment that belonged to us. You, too, can receive this perfect love and forgiveness for your wrong deeds. The punishment has already been borne on your behalf--do you want to receive the freedom?